{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KRYc57rkrVBm7E7bohHHnW","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# GitHub Actions Manager\n     2→\n     3→A beautiful dashboard for monitoring and managing GitHub Actions workflows across your organizations.\n     4→\n     5→## Features\n     6→\n     7→- **Organization Selection**: Switch between your personal account and any organization you belong to\n     8→- **Real-time Monitoring**: Auto-refresh every 30 seconds with live status indicators\n     9→- **Workflow Overview**: See all workflow runs across all repositories in one view\n    10→- **Status Filtering**: Filter by running, queued, successful, or failed workflows\n    11→- **Job Details**: Expand any workflow to see individual job steps and their status\n    12→- **Build Logs**: View build logs directly in the dashboard\n    13→- **Quick Actions**: Re-run workflows, retry failed jobs, or cancel running builds with one click\n    14→- **Responsive Design**: Works great on desktop and mobile\n    15→\n    16→## Tech Stack\n    17→\n    18→- **Framework**: Next.js 14 with App Router\n    19→- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui components\n    20→- **Authentication**: NextAuth.js with GitHub OAuth\n    21→- **API Client**: Octokit (official GitHub SDK)\n    22→- **Language**: TypeScript\n    23→\n    24→## Setup\n    25→\n    26→### 1. Create a GitHub OAuth App\n    27→\n    28→1. Go to [GitHub Developer Settings](https://github.com/settings/developers)\n    29→2. Click \"New OAuth App\"\n    30→3. Fill in the details (adjust port as needed):\n    31→   - **Application name**: GitHub Actions Manager (or your preferred name)\n    32→   - **Homepage URL**: `http://localhost:YOUR_PORT`\n    33→   - **Authorization callback URL**: `http://localhost:YOUR_PORT/api/auth/callback/github`\n    34→4. Click \"Register application\"\n    35→5. Generate a new client secret\n    36→\n    37→### 2. Configure Environment Variables\n    38→\n    39→Copy the example environment file:\n    40→\n    41→```bash\n    42→cp .env.example .env.local\n    43→```\n    44→\n    45→Edit `.env.local` with your values:\n    46→\n    47→```env\n    48→GITHUB_ID=your_github_oauth_app_client_id\n    49→GITHUB_SECRET=your_github_oauth_app_client_secret\n    50→NEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:YOUR_PORT\n    51→NEXTAUTH_SECRET=your_random_secret_string\n    52→```\n    53→\n    54→Generate a secure NEXTAUTH_SECRET:\n    55→\n    56→```bash\n    57→openssl rand -base64 32\n    58→```\n    59→\n    60→### 3. Install Dependencies\n    61→\n    62→```bash\n    63→npm install\n    64→```\n    65→\n    66→### 4. Run the Development Server\n    67→\n    68→```bash\n    69→npm run dev -- -p YOUR_PORT\n    70→```\n    71→\n    72→Open `http://localhost:YOUR_PORT` in your browser.\n    73→\n    74→## Usage\n    75→\n    76→1. Click \"Sign in with GitHub\" on the landing page\n    77→2. Authorize the app to access your GitHub account\n    78→3. Select an organization or your personal account from the dropdown\n    79→4. Browse your workflow runs, filter by status, and manage your builds\n    80→\n    81→## Required GitHub Permissions\n    82→\n    83→The app requests the following OAuth scopes:\n    84→\n    85→- `read:user` - Read user profile information\n    86→- `user:email` - Access email addresses\n    87→- `read:org` - List organizations\n    88→- `repo` - Access repositories (needed for workflow management)\n    89→- `workflow` - Manage workflow runs\n    90→\n    91→## Development\n    92→\n    93→```bash\n    94→# Run development server\n    95→npm run dev\n    96→\n    97→# Build for production\n    98→npm run build\n    99→\n   100→# Start production server\n   101→npm start\n   102→\n   103→# Type check\n   104→npx tsc --noEmit\n   105→```\n   106→\n   107→## Deployment\n   108→\n   109→This app can be deployed to any platform that supports Next.js:\n   110→\n   111→- [Vercel](https://vercel.com) (recommended)\n   112→- [Netlify](https://netlify.com)\n   113→- [Railway](https://railway.app)\n   114→- Self-hosted with Docker\n   115→\n   116→Remember to update the OAuth callback URL in your GitHub OAuth App settings to match your production URL.\n   117→\n   118→## License\n   119→\n   120→MIT\n   121→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CZkSgrsB2gphzcmXCNrW1W","session_id":"bc23d326-89d9-481a-b0c0-3e13574a6bab","uuid":"ae6414e4-b737-4457-84e7-472b20e7ca34"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CZkSgrsB2gphzcmXCNrW1W","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the codebase. Let me compile a detailed report with all the findings.\n\n## Comprehensive Codebase Analysis: GitHub Actions Manager\n\n### 1. **What is this project? What does it do?**\n\nGitHub Actions Manager is a web-based dashboard application that helps developers monitor, manage, and control GitHub Actions workflows across their GitHub organizations and personal repositories. Users can view real-time workflow status, filter runs by status, manage job execution, view logs, and trigger actions like re-runs or cancellations—all from a unified, modern interface.\n\n**Key Value Propositions:**\n- Real-time monitoring of all workflow runs across multiple repositories\n- Centralized dashboard to manage Actions across entire organizations\n- Quick action controls (rerun, retry failed jobs, cancel runs)\n- Live auto-refresh every 30 seconds\n- Beautiful, responsive UI with detailed job insights\n\n### 2. **Technologies/Frameworks**\n\n**Core Stack:**\n- **Frontend Framework**: Next.js 16.1.4 (App Router)\n- **UI Framework**: React 19.2.3\n- **Language**: TypeScript 5\n- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS 4 with @tailwindcss/postcss\n- **Component Library**: shadcn/ui (Radix UI primitives)\n- **Icons**: lucide-react\n- **Date Handling**: date-fns 4.1.0\n- **Authentication**: NextAuth.js 4.24.13 with GitHub OAuth\n- **API Client**: @octokit/rest 22.0.1 (official GitHub SDK)\n- **Utility Libraries**: clsx, class-variance-authority, tailwind-merge\n- **Dev Tools**: ESLint 9, TypeScript\n\n**Build & Runtime:**\n- **Package Manager**: npm\n- **Server Runtime**: Node.js (via Next.js)\n- **Configuration**: ESLint (type-aware), PostCSS, TypeScript\n\n### 3. **Project Structure**\n\n```\n/src\n  /app\n    /api\n      /auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts          # NextAuth.js configuration\n      /github\n        /orgs/route.ts                       # Get orgs list\n        /user/route.ts                       # Get authenticated user\n        /repos/[org]/route.ts                # Get repos for org\n        /runs/[org]/route.ts                 # Get workflow runs for org\n        /runs/[org]/summaries/route.ts       # Get job summaries (batch)\n        /runs/[org]/[repo]/[runId]/\n          /rerun/route.ts                    # Rerun workflow\n          /rerun-failed/route.ts             # Retry failed jobs\n          /cancel/route.ts                   # Cancel workflow\n        /jobs/[owner]/[repo]/[runId]/route.ts     # Get workflow jobs\n        /logs/[owner]/[repo]/[jobId]/route.ts     # Download job logs\n    /layout.tsx                              # Root layout with providers\n    /page.tsx                                # Landing page + dashboard\n    /globals.css                             # Global styles\n  \n  /components\n    /ui                                      # shadcn/ui components\n      button.tsx, badge.tsx, card.tsx, dialog.tsx, dropdown-menu.tsx\n      select.tsx, tabs.tsx, avatar.tsx, tooltip.tsx, collapsible.tsx\n      alert.tsx, separator.tsx, scroll-area.tsx, skeleton.tsx\n    /auth\n      login-button.tsx                       # GitHub sign-in button\n    /dashboard\n      workflow-dashboard.tsx                 # Main dashboard component (1000+ lines)\n      workflow-run-card.tsx                  # Individual run display\n      workflow-jobs.tsx                      # Job details component\n      org-selector.tsx                       # Organization dropdown\n      status-badge.tsx                       # Status visual indicator\n    /providers\n      session-provider.tsx                   # NextAuth SessionProvider wrapper\n\n  /lib\n    auth.ts                                  # NextAuth configuration\n    github.ts                                # GitHub API helpers + type definitions\n    github-route-error.ts                    # API error handling & rate limit extraction\n    api-error.ts                             # Error response types & utilities\n    workflow-status.ts                       # Workflow status utilities\n    utils.ts                                 # Utility functions (cn for className merging)\n\n  /types\n    next-auth.d.ts                           # NextAuth session type augmentation\n\nConfiguration Files:\n  tsconfig.json                              # TypeScript compiler options\n  next.config.ts                             # Next.js configuration (empty)\n  components.json                            # shadcn/ui configuration\n  postcss.config.mjs                         # PostCSS/Tailwind config\n  eslint.config.mjs                          # ESLint configuration\n  package.json                               # Dependencies & scripts\n```\n\n### 4. **Main Features**\n\n**User-Facing Features:**\n1. **Authentication**: GitHub OAuth login with personal + org support\n2. **Organization/Account Selection**: Dropdown to switch between personal repos and organizations\n3. **Workflow Monitoring**: Real-time list of all workflow runs with auto-refresh every 30 seconds\n4. **Status Filtering**: Filter by running, queued, successful, or failed\n5. **Sorting**: Priority-based, newest first, oldest first, duration, queue age, repository\n6. **Grouping**: Group runs by repository, workflow, branch, or status\n7. **View Modes**: Card view or \"ops\" (operations) view with compact table layout\n8. **Job Details**: Expand runs to see individual job status and step details\n9. **Build Logs**: Access job logs directly\n10. **Quick Actions**: Rerun, retry failed jobs, cancel runs\n11. **Job Summaries**: Batch loading of job summaries with progress indicators\n12. **Rate Limit Handling**: Graceful handling of GitHub API rate limits with retry timing\n13. **Statistics**: Real-time counts of total, running, queued, successful, failed runs\n14. **Responsive Design**: Works on desktop and mobile\n\n**Developer Features:**\n1. TypeScript strict mode throughout\n2. Comprehensive error handling with rate limit detection\n3. Batch processing for API calls (groups of 10 repos, 6 job summaries)\n4. Proper session management and authorization checks\n5. Client-side + server-side state management\n\n### 5. **Configuration**\n\n**Environment Variables Required:**\n```env\nGITHUB_ID=<OAuth app client ID>\nGITHUB_SECRET=<OAuth app client secret>\nNEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:port\nNEXTAUTH_SECRET=<random secret for session signing>\n```\n\n**NextAuth Configuration** (src/lib/auth.ts):\n- Provider: GitHub OAuth\n- Scopes: `read:user user:email read:org repo workflow`\n- JWT callbacks for token management\n- Session augmentation with access token\n\n**TypeScript Config**:\n- Target: ES2017\n- Strict mode enabled\n- Path aliases: `@/*` → `./src/*`\n- JSX: react-jsx\n- Module resolution: bundler\n\n**Tailwind CSS**:\n- Tailwind v4 with CSS-based configuration\n- Custom color theme via CSS variables\n- Components built with Radix UI primitives\n\n### 6. **UI Components**\n\n**Shadcn/ui Components Used:**\n- Button, Badge, Card, Dialog\n- DropdownMenu, Select, Tabs\n- Avatar, Tooltip, Collapsible\n- Alert, Separator, ScrollArea, Skeleton\n\n**Custom Components:**\n- **WorkflowDashboard**: Main orchestrator (1000+ lines, state management, filtering, sorting, grouping, auto-refresh)\n- **WorkflowRunCard**: Displays individual workflow run with expandable job details and actions\n- **WorkflowJobs**: Shows job details with step-level status\n- **OrgSelector**: Organization/account dropdown with avatar\n- **StatusBadge**: Visual status indicator\n- **LoginButton**: GitHub auth button\n\n**UI Features:**\n- Dark mode support (CSS variables)\n- Responsive grid layout\n- Skeleton loaders\n- Status-based color coding (red for failure, green for success, blue for running, amber for queued)\n- Animated spinners for loading states\n- Toast-like status notifications for actions\n\n### 7. **API Routes and Server-Side Logic**\n\n**Authentication Flow:**\n- `GET /api/auth/[...nextauth]` - NextAuth.js handler (signin, callback, session)\n\n**Organization & Account Management:**\n- `GET /api/github/orgs` - List user's orgs + personal account with metadata\n- `GET /api/github/repos/[org]` - List repos for org (with pagination, 500 max)\n- `GET /api/github/user` - Get authenticated user info\n\n**Workflow Data:**\n- `GET /api/github/runs/[org]` - Get all workflow runs for org (batch fetches repos, then runs)\n- `POST /api/github/runs/[org]/summaries` - Batch fetch job summaries for multiple runs\n\n**Workflow Actions:**\n- `POST /api/github/runs/[org]/[repo]/[runId]/rerun` - Re-run entire workflow\n- `POST /api/github/runs/[org]/[repo]/[runId]/rerun-failed` - Retry failed jobs only\n- `POST /api/github/runs/[org]/[repo]/[runId]/cancel` - Cancel running workflow\n\n**Job Details:**\n- `GET /api/github/jobs/[owner]/[repo]/[runId]` - Get jobs for a workflow run\n- `GET /api/github/logs/[owner]/[repo]/[jobId]` - Download job logs\n\n**Key Implementation Details:**\n- All routes require authentication (401 if missing access token)\n- Batch processing to avoid rate limits (10 repos at a time, 6 job summaries at a time)\n- Error extraction includes GitHub rate limit headers (x-ratelimit-remaining, x-ratelimit-reset)\n- Proper HTTP status codes (429 for rate limit, 500 for errors)\n- Server-side session access via NextAuth's `getServerSession`\n\n### 8. **Authentication/Authorization**\n\n**Authentication Method**: GitHub OAuth 2.0 via NextAuth.js\n\n**Flow:**\n1. User clicks \"Sign in with GitHub\"\n2. Redirected to GitHub authorization\n3. GitHub callback to `/api/auth/callback/github`\n4. NextAuth creates JWT with access token\n5. Access token stored in session for API calls\n\n**Authorization Checks:**\n- Every API route checks for `session?.accessToken`\n- Returns 401 Unauthorized if missing\n- OAuth scopes ensure appropriate permissions:\n  - `read:user` - profile info\n  - `user:email` - email access\n  - `read:org` - list organizations\n  - `repo` - repository access\n  - `workflow` - workflow management\n\n**Session Management:**\n- NextAuth.js JWT strategy\n- Access token persisted in JWT callback\n- Session includes access token for use in Octokit calls\n\n### 9. **Database/Data Storage**\n\n**No persistent database** - This is a stateless application.\n\n**Data Sources:**\n- GitHub API (real-time queries via Octokit)\n- Browser session storage (Next.js/NextAuth)\n- Component state (React hooks)\n\n**Data Flow:**\n1. Frontend fetches from API routes\n2. API routes call GitHub API via Octokit\n3. Data returned as JSON, stored in component state\n4. State invalidation triggers refetches\n\n**Caching Strategy:**\n- No explicit caching - requests use `cache: \"no-store\"`\n- Auto-refresh every 30 seconds for live data\n- Batching to minimize API calls (repos, summaries)\n\n### 10. **Deployment**\n\n**Supported Platforms:**\n- Vercel (recommended - native Next.js support)\n- Netlify\n- Railway\n- Self-hosted with Docker\n\n**Deployment Requirements:**\n- Node.js runtime\n- Environment variables (GITHUB_*, NEXTAUTH_*)\n- Update OAuth callback URL in GitHub app settings\n\n**Build Process:**\n```bash\nnpm run build  # Outputs .next directory\nnpm start      # Runs production server\n```\n\n**Notes:**\n- Stateless (no database needed)\n- Can be deployed as serverless (Vercel) or traditional server\n- Session data managed by NextAuth (can use default MemoryStore or configure external store)\n\n### 11. **Available Scripts**\n\nFrom package.json:\n```bash\nnpm run dev      # Start development server with hot reload\nnpm run build    # Build for production\nnpm start        # Start production server\nnpm run lint     # Run ESLint\n```\n\nDevelopment setup:\n```bash\nnpm install      # Install dependencies\nnpm run dev -- -p PORT  # Run on specific port\n```\n\n### 12. **CI/CD Configuration**\n\n**No CI/CD configuration found** in the repository. The project includes:\n- ESLint for code quality (`npm run lint`)\n- TypeScript for type checking (implicit in build)\n- No GitHub Actions workflows in the repo itself\n\n**Recommended additions:**\n- GitHub Actions for testing/linting on PRs\n- Automated deployment via Vercel or similar\n\n### 13. **Key Dependencies**\n\n**Production Dependencies:**\n- `next` (16.1.4) - React framework\n- `react` & `react-dom` (19.2.3) - UI library\n- `next-auth` (4.24.13) - Authentication\n- `@octokit/rest` (22.0.1) - GitHub API client\n- `tailwindcss` (4) - CSS framework\n- `@radix-ui/*` (various) - Accessible component primitives\n- `lucide-react` (0.562.0) - Icon library\n- `date-fns` (4.1.0) - Date utilities\n- `clsx` & `tailwind-merge` - CSS utility helpers\n\n**Dev Dependencies:**\n- `typescript` (5) - Type checking\n- `eslint` (9) - Code linting\n- `@tailwindcss/postcss` (4) - PostCSS plugin\n- `@types/*` packages - Type definitions\n\n**Notable Patterns:**\n- Radix UI used extensively (primitive component library)\n- shadcn/ui for pre-styled components\n- Proper TypeScript types for GitHub API responses\n- Error handling with rate limit awareness\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n**GitHub Actions Manager** is a modern, full-stack TypeScript web application built with Next.js that provides a unified dashboard for monitoring and managing GitHub Actions workflows. It leverages:\n\n- **Frontend**: React 19 with Server Components, Tailwind CSS, and shadcn/ui\n- **Backend**: Next.js API routes with server-side authentication\n- **Authentication**: GitHub OAuth via NextAuth.js\n- **External APIs**: GitHub REST API via Octokit\n- **State Management**: React hooks (no Redux/Zustand)\n- **Code Quality**: TypeScript strict mode, ESLint\n\nThe application is **stateless** (no database), **production-ready**, with thoughtful UX patterns like batching API calls, rate limit handling, and intelligent auto-refresh. It demonstrates solid software engineering practices with proper error handling, authorization checks, and type safety throughout."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a7d0807 (for resuming to continue this agent's work if needed)\n<usage>total_tokens: 62962\ntool_uses: 36\nduration_ms: 68595</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc23d326-89d9-481a-b0c0-3e13574a6bab","uuid":"18cb6dca-6048-4c80-92a8-afc58f12ea3c","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Thoroughly explore the codebase at /home/dave/projects/github-actions-manager/.worktrees/feat-readme-build-it to understand:\n\n1. What is this project? What does it do?\n2. What technologies/frameworks does it use?\n3. What is the project structure (all directories and key files)?\n4. What are the main features?\n5. How is it configured (package.json, next.config, tsconfig, etc.)?\n6. What UI components exist?\n7. What API routes or server-side logic exists?\n8. What authentication/authorization is used?\n9. What database or data storage is used?\n10. How is it deployed?\n11. What scripts are available (build, test, lint, etc.)?\n12. Any CI/CD configuration?\n13. What are the key dependencies?\n\nBe very thorough - read package.json, next.config files, app directory structure, component files, API routes, middleware, etc. I need a comprehensive understanding to write a README.","agentId":"a7d0807","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the codebase. Let me compile a detailed report with all the findings.\n\n## Comprehensive Codebase Analysis: GitHub Actions Manager\n\n### 1. **What is this project? What does it do?**\n\nGitHub Actions Manager is a web-based dashboard application that helps developers monitor, manage, and control GitHub Actions workflows across their GitHub organizations and personal repositories. Users can view real-time workflow status, filter runs by status, manage job execution, view logs, and trigger actions like re-runs or cancellations—all from a unified, modern in