{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0157xS9WbwA5Q2xixWjDrtTa","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# GitHub Actions Manager\n     2→\n     3→A beautiful dashboard for monitoring and managing GitHub Actions workflows across your organizations.\n     4→\n     5→## Features\n     6→\n     7→- **Organization Selection**: Switch between your personal account and any organization you belong to\n     8→- **Real-time Monitoring**: Auto-refresh every 30 seconds with live status indicators\n     9→- **Workflow Overview**: See all workflow runs across all repositories in one view\n    10→- **Status Filtering**: Filter by running, queued, successful, or failed workflows\n    11→- **Job Details**: Expand any workflow to see individual job steps and their status\n    12→- **Build Logs**: View build logs directly in the dashboard\n    13→- **Quick Actions**: Re-run workflows, retry failed jobs, or cancel running builds with one click\n    14→- **Responsive Design**: Works great on desktop and mobile\n    15→\n    16→## Tech Stack\n    17→\n    18→- **Framework**: Next.js 14 with App Router\n    19→- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui components\n    20→- **Authentication**: NextAuth.js with GitHub OAuth\n    21→- **API Client**: Octokit (official GitHub SDK)\n    22→- **Language**: TypeScript\n    23→\n    24→## Setup\n    25→\n    26→### 1. Create a GitHub OAuth App\n    27→\n    28→1. Go to [GitHub Developer Settings](https://github.com/settings/developers)\n    29→2. Click \"New OAuth App\"\n    30→3. Fill in the details (adjust port as needed):\n    31→   - **Application name**: GitHub Actions Manager (or your preferred name)\n    32→   - **Homepage URL**: `http://localhost:YOUR_PORT`\n    33→   - **Authorization callback URL**: `http://localhost:YOUR_PORT/api/auth/callback/github`\n    34→4. Click \"Register application\"\n    35→5. Generate a new client secret\n    36→\n    37→### 2. Configure Environment Variables\n    38→\n    39→Copy the example environment file:\n    40→\n    41→```bash\n    42→cp .env.example .env.local\n    43→```\n    44→\n    45→Edit `.env.local` with your values:\n    46→\n    47→```env\n    48→GITHUB_ID=your_github_oauth_app_client_id\n    49→GITHUB_SECRET=your_github_oauth_app_client_secret\n    50→NEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:YOUR_PORT\n    51→NEXTAUTH_SECRET=your_random_secret_string\n    52→```\n    53→\n    54→Generate a secure NEXTAUTH_SECRET:\n    55→\n    56→```bash\n    57→openssl rand -base64 32\n    58→```\n    59→\n    60→### 3. Install Dependencies\n    61→\n    62→```bash\n    63→npm install\n    64→```\n    65→\n    66→### 4. Run the Development Server\n    67→\n    68→```bash\n    69→npm run dev -- -p YOUR_PORT\n    70→```\n    71→\n    72→Open `http://localhost:YOUR_PORT` in your browser.\n    73→\n    74→## Usage\n    75→\n    76→1. Click \"Sign in with GitHub\" on the landing page\n    77→2. Authorize the app to access your GitHub account\n    78→3. Select an organization or your personal account from the dropdown\n    79→4. Browse your workflow runs, filter by status, and manage your builds\n    80→\n    81→## Required GitHub Permissions\n    82→\n    83→The app requests the following OAuth scopes:\n    84→\n    85→- `read:user` - Read user profile information\n    86→- `user:email` - Access email addresses\n    87→- `read:org` - List organizations\n    88→- `repo` - Access repositories (needed for workflow management)\n    89→- `workflow` - Manage workflow runs\n    90→\n    91→## Development\n    92→\n    93→```bash\n    94→# Run development server\n    95→npm run dev\n    96→\n    97→# Build for production\n    98→npm run build\n    99→\n   100→# Start production server\n   101→npm start\n   102→\n   103→# Type check\n   104→npx tsc --noEmit\n   105→```\n   106→\n   107→## Deployment\n   108→\n   109→This app can be deployed to any platform that supports Next.js:\n   110→\n   111→- [Vercel](https://vercel.com) (recommended)\n   112→- [Netlify](https://netlify.com)\n   113→- [Railway](https://railway.app)\n   114→- Self-hosted with Docker\n   115→\n   116→Remember to update the OAuth callback URL in your GitHub OAuth App settings to match your production URL.\n   117→\n   118→## License\n   119→\n   120→MIT\n   121→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc23d326-89d9-481a-b0c0-3e13574a6bab","uuid":"e6acb84d-8fa0-4294-8aff-f7c835ab9e4a","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/dave/projects/github-actions-manager/.worktrees/feat-readme-build-it/README.md","content":"# GitHub Actions Manager\n\nA beautiful dashboard for monitoring and managing GitHub Actions workflows across your organizations.\n\n## Features\n\n- **Organization Selection**: Switch between your personal account and any organization you belong to\n- **Real-time Monitoring**: Auto-refresh every 30 seconds with live status indicators\n- **Workflow Overview**: See all workflow runs across all repositories in one view\n- **Status Filtering**: Filter by running, queued, successful, or failed workflows\n- **Job Details**: Expand any workflow to see individual job steps and their status\n- **Build Logs**: View build logs directly in the dashboard\n- **Quick Actions**: Re-run workflows, retry failed jobs, or cancel running builds with one click\n- **Responsive Design**: Works great on desktop and mobile\n\n## Tech Stack\n\n- **Framework**: Next.js 14 with App Router\n- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui components\n- **Authentication**: NextAuth.js with GitHub OAuth\n- **API Client**: Octokit (official GitHub SDK)\n- **Language**: TypeScript\n\n## Setup\n\n### 1. Create a GitHub OAuth App\n\n1. Go to [GitHub Developer Settings](https://github.com/settings/developers)\n2. Click \"New OAuth App\"\n3. Fill in the details (adjust port as needed):\n   - **Application name**: GitHub Actions Manager (or your preferred name)\n   - **Homepage URL**: `http://localhost:YOUR_PORT`\n   - **Authorization callback URL**: `http://localhost:YOUR_PORT/api/auth/callback/github`\n4. Click \"Register application\"\n5. Generate a new client secret\n\n### 2. Configure Environment Variables\n\nCopy the example environment file:\n\n```bash\ncp .env.example .env.local\n```\n\nEdit `.env.local` with your values:\n\n```env\nGITHUB_ID=your_github_oauth_app_client_id\nGITHUB_SECRET=your_github_oauth_app_client_secret\nNEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:YOUR_PORT\nNEXTAUTH_SECRET=your_random_secret_string\n```\n\nGenerate a secure NEXTAUTH_SECRET:\n\n```bash\nopenssl rand -base64 32\n```\n\n### 3. Install Dependencies\n\n```bash\nnpm install\n```\n\n### 4. Run the Development Server\n\n```bash\nnpm run dev -- -p YOUR_PORT\n```\n\nOpen `http://localhost:YOUR_PORT` in your browser.\n\n## Usage\n\n1. Click \"Sign in with GitHub\" on the landing page\n2. Authorize the app to access your GitHub account\n3. Select an organization or your personal account from the dropdown\n4. Browse your workflow runs, filter by status, and manage your builds\n\n## Required GitHub Permissions\n\nThe app requests the following OAuth scopes:\n\n- `read:user` - Read user profile information\n- `user:email` - Access email addresses\n- `read:org` - List organizations\n- `repo` - Access repositories (needed for workflow management)\n- `workflow` - Manage workflow runs\n\n## Development\n\n```bash\n# Run development server\nnpm run dev\n\n# Build for production\nnpm run build\n\n# Start production server\nnpm start\n\n# Type check\nnpx tsc --noEmit\n```\n\n## Deployment\n\nThis app can be deployed to any platform that supports Next.js:\n\n- [Vercel](https://vercel.com) (recommended)\n- [Netlify](https://netlify.com)\n- [Railway](https://railway.app)\n- Self-hosted with Docker\n\nRemember to update the OAuth callback URL in your GitHub OAuth App settings to match your production URL.\n\n## License\n\nMIT\n","numLines":121,"startLine":1,"totalLines":121}}}
